        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-14T00:23:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So IIUC between l.9816 and l.9817 the local DNS server is actually blocked. Should we do it the other way around (allow port 53 on this subnet first, THEN block the rest) to avoid a race condition where a packet that should be allowed is blocked ?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9817,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 9817,
        "endChar": 36
      },
      "revId": "049d3b4f58d9bc33aecc9c68c93e92c07acc8501",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "483e3464_cc6a2330",
        "filename": "service/src/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 9817,
      "author": {
        "id": 3485374
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-15T23:01:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good catch. But the problem with reversing l.9817 and l.9816 is that we can\u0027t check if the dns server is local or not. \nThe current order is \n1. Populate the LocalNetAccessMap with all prefixes.\n2. for all dns servers (let n), check if any dns matches with any existing prefixes in the map to know whether it is local. [TC \u003d O(1)*n because of trie map]\n3. If found in any existing prefix, then add allow rule for it.\n\nOne solution to this can be updating dns on every prefix addition. Just before adding the prefix to LocalNetAccessMap, check if the prefix contains a dns and add dns allow rule first and then prefix block rule. Same with removing.\nThis will make the time complexity m*n. \n\nAs discussed offline, I\u0027m going with this approach.",
      "parentUuid": "cbbf9da0_c2ee54b1",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9817,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 9817,
        "endChar": 36
      },
      "revId": "049d3b4f58d9bc33aecc9c68c93e92c07acc8501",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
